I’ll inspect the specimen metadata, pattern, frozen index, and the supplied GSAS-II workflow first. Then I’ll build and refine the smallest physically supported phase set, run the required one-time bounded polish, and export everything from the accepted project.

The chemistry is a ZnO + WO₃ firing at 700 °C, so ZnWO₄ is the natural principal candidate, with residual oxides or an off-stoichiometric tungstate still possible. I’m now checking the supplied refinement constraints and the frozen COD subset before assigning peaks; I’ll treat any unindexed reflection family as evidence for an additional phase rather than forcing the principal phase to absorb it.

The dominant reflection family matches wolframite-type ZnWO₄ very closely: the diagnostic low-angle peaks near 19.2°, 24.1/24.8°, the 30.98° maximum, and the 36.7°, 41.55°, 49–54°, and 62–65° groups all occur in the expected P2/c sequence. A CoMoO₄ wolframite analogue is the best physical start so far after Co→Zn and Mo→W substitution; I’m keeping a second independent wolframite start for a basin check and testing whether the smaller/narrower features require residual ZnO or WO₃.

The one-phase model explains every coherent reflection family; the remaining residuals sit on ZnWO₄ peaks (center/wing and intensity errors), not at a separate set of d-spacings. Trial inclusion of the second calibration term drove both zero and specimen shift to their allowed bounds for only a 0.07-point Rwp gain, so I’m discarding that correlated branch and retaining the interior, shift-only calibration. The isotropic microstrain term likewise pinned at its lower bound and will be removed rather than reported as evidence.

The equally developed parent comparison is now decisive: the CoMoO₄-derived wolframite basin gives Rwp 17.96% with an interior [111] March–Dollase ratio (0.708), while FeNbO₄, InNbO₄, InTaO₄, and MnReO₄ starts remain at 18.53–20.67%; the latter two also pin texture at the allowed limit. I’m retaining the CoMoO₄ basin. Before final profile work, I’m explicitly testing the weak residual sequence against wurtzite ZnO and the plausible WO₃ polymorphs, because several small features near 32°, 47.9°, 56.8°, and 62.8° could form a ZnO family.

The phase screen confirms a real second family: monoclinic WO₃ drops Rwp from 17.61% to about 14% at a stable positive scale (~7 wt% at the first checkpoint), far more than ZnO (0.15-point gain and a size term immediately pinned at 0.04 µm). I’m completing the equal cell/size development of the credible WO₃ settings now; ZnO is currently unsupported as a retained phase.

The complete three-phase model is now stable and chemically coherent: ZnWO₄ + residual monoclinic WO₃ + residual wurtzite ZnO refines to 84.63/10.00/5.37 wt%, with all three positive scales stable, interior cell/size parameters, and Rwp 11.02%. The WO₃ P2₁/c basin beats the triclinic alternative at an equally developed checkpoint; the triclinic cell also pins two metric terms, so it is discarded. I’m now addressing the remaining center/wing residuals with the permitted phase-broadening models before the one-time final polish.

The three-phase solution is chemically and crystallographically closed. I’m correcting one confounded profile-term screen, then I’ll test whether either minor phase has independently supported texture before locking the terminal pre-polish model.

The corrected screen supports polarization at 0.881 (interior, ΔRwp ≈ 0.10 percentage point). Axial divergence changes Rwp by only 0.002 and roughness runs to supplied bounds, so I’m retaining polarization and rejecting those two nuisance refinements.

Both residual phases show modest but coherent texture: WO₃ favors [100] (ratio 1.126), while ZnO favors its physically natural [001] c-axis (0.854). Competing axes either fit less well or hit a bound. I’ll now combine those interior solutions and re-settle the full model in the prescribed parameter order.

The joint model drove only the dominant-phase texture to the tested lower bound. I’m rolling that refinement back to its independently supported interior checkpoint (0.70565) while keeping the term fixed; all calibration, profile, WO₃/ZnO texture, and polarization parameters remained interior.

The remaining large feature near 41.6° is not an unindexed peak family: it is paired with excess calculated intensity on adjacent accepted reflections. I’m checking its reflection assignment now; that distinction determines whether the bounded coordinate polish can legitimately resolve it.

The 41.56° and 41.81° pair are both ZnWO₄ reflections whose calculated intensities are reversed; their positions are already correct. The earlier analogue-coordinate path stopped exactly at its first 0.10 Å oxygen bound, so I’m preserving that boundary geometry as a second physical start and comparing it against the original CoMoO₄-derived start at equal bounded checkpoints.

Both independent starts converged to the same oxygen basin and improved Rwp by about 0.54 point, but the same O3 coordinate again reached the edge of its local 0.10 Å window with no short contacts. I’m continuing that shared physical basin from the common endpoint until the coordinate becomes interior or geometry fails.

The continued basin is now interior and contact-free: O3 moved ~0.19 Å from the CoMoO₄ analogue, while the other ZnWO₄ coordinates converged consistently from both starts. This resolves much of the swapped-intensity residual. I’m re-settling all accepted cells, profile, and specimen terms around that structural model now.

The refined oxygen geometry remains chemically plausible but does not explain the 41.58° intensity, so I’m treating that peak as genuinely unassigned rather than forcing the ZnWO₄ coordinates. I’ll rank the short reaction-compatible set—other WOₓ/ZnOₓ structures and symmetry-distinct precursor polymorphs—against the entire weighted residual family, then develop only serious matches.

The residual-family ranking points first to W₃O₈, with the P2₁/m WO₃ polymorph as the only comparably plausible oxidized alternative; W₂O₇ is chemically invalid for tungsten and ZnO₂ is unstable at 700 °C. I’m comparing both available W₃O₈ structures plus P2₁/m WO₃ at the same GSAS-II checkpoint, and retaining only positive, family-wide contributions.

Neither W₃O₈ reference contributes stably (≤0.1 wt%, one cell term and size hit bounds), while P2₁/m WO₃ gives only a 0.3 wt% scale-only improvement before numerical failure. That is insufficient to assign the family. I’m therefore testing the remaining balanced possibility: a second ZnWO₄ topology, using symmetry-diverse ABO₄ parents from substituted chemistries and identical target-volume adaptation.

The broad parent screen is still processing the 418 frozen ABO₄ references and both cation mappings. So far this is only a topology ranking—no candidate has been added to the model or allowed to absorb intensity.

One result is structurally important: the residual is strongly matched by the same P2/c wolframite framework with the Zn and W sites interchanged. Because both sites are octahedral, I’m testing this as an alternative ordered basin—not adding a correlated duplicate phase—to determine whether the original site assignment itself is wrong.

The alternative ordered site assignment is decisive: at identical phase inventory and nuisance development it reduces Rwp from 10.15% to 5.71%, while all fractions remain stable and every retained term stays interior. I’m now validating that inverse-wolframite basin against additional parent-coordinate starts and its local bond geometry before accepting it.

All five inverse-parent starts converge to the same joint intensity basin (Rwp 4.424% before cell settling). The FeNbO₄-derived start leaves all oxygen coordinates interior and reaches the lowest equal checkpoint, while the others retain one or more local coordinate-bound atoms. I’m fully settling every branch before making the basin choice, so the provisional 0.2-point spread is not being used prematurely.

The fully settled comparison confirms the FeNbO₄-derived inverse basin as the physical winner: Rwp 3.818%, versus 4.010–4.415% for the other starts. Its oxygen sites are interior and contact-free; the one Fe-start cation-window edge is independently bracketed by the Co/In/Ta starts at the same coordinate, so it is not an unresolved boundary solution. The other basins are discarded.

The inverse model closes the previously unassigned 41.6° family. The largest remaining residuals sit on accepted ZnO/WO₃ reflections rather than forming a new lattice family. Before calling that profile closure, I’m rechecking all three texture axes in the accepted structural basin; coordinate changes can otherwise make an inherited texture choice look artificially favorable.

The re-screen changes one accepted term: WO₃ [110] texture is now clearly superior and interior (ratio 1.3605; Rwp 3.301% versus 3.818% for [100]). ZnWO₄ remains uniquely [111], and ZnO retains the slightly better, physically natural [001] axis, though its axis choice is weakly distinguished. I’m rebuilding the terminal model with that corrected WO₃ term.

The remaining profile is already close to counting noise, but a few accepted high-angle reflections still show width-sign residuals. I’m doing one bounded phase-by-phase microstrain screen now; any branch that returns to 300 ppm will be rejected as the supplied lower-bound result.

Only WO₃ isotropic microstrain is potentially supported: 325 ppm, interior, with ΔRwp ≈ 0.015. Every ZnWO₄/ZnO strain branch and every uniaxial branch hits a lower bound. I’m testing that single WO₃ term in the full joint model; it will be retained only if it remains interior after all accepted profile terms are re-settled.

The WO₃ strain survives the full model at 1819 ± 97 ppm and improves Rwp to 3.192%; its strongest correlation with WO₃ size is 0.874, substantial but still separately determined. I’m retaining it provisionally and doing the last profile audit on the previously fixed U and X terms, with W, sizes, and WO₃ strain equally re-developed in every branch.

The audit supports U: it converges to 11.71 with ΔRwp ≈ 0.073 and only modest correlation (|r|max 0.31 to W). X produces no measurable gain and is strongly entangled with W/size, so X stays fixed at 1.0. I’m incorporating U and re-running the complete terminal sequence once more.

I’ve finished the phase-assignment comparisons and am closing the terminal refinement now. The accepted model is inverse-ordered wolframite ZnWO₄ with monoclinic WO₃ and wurtzite ZnO; I’m checking the last supported profile term and replay stability before the one permitted bounded polish.

The terminal model now replays cleanly: the joint refinement converged with maximum shift/error 0.042, no frozen parameters, and Rwp 3.116%. The crystalline fractions are already stable at roughly 83.24% ZnWO₄, 11.88% WO₃, and 4.88% ZnO; I’m doing the final bound/correlation and residual-family audit next.

The terminal audit is physical and bounded: every retained specimen/profile term is interior, all phase scales are positive, no short contacts are present, and the remaining residual maxima do not form a consistent unindexed reflection family. The WO₃ size/strain pair is correlated (0.88) but remains separately determined (about 2.3% and 6.3% relative uncertainty), so I’m retaining it. I’m now invoking the supplied bounded polish exactly once; afterward I will only replay-check and export this selected branch.

The polish is still running its three prescribed coordinate paths. The first completed path converged without frozen parameters and improved Rwp to 2.929%; several WO₃ coordinates that initially met their ±0.10 Å limits were automatically restored before the accepted interior-only settle, exactly as the audit procedure requires.

The prescribed audit selected the phasewise branch at Rwp 2.9287%. It is eligible, converged (maximum shift/error 0.0119), has no frozen/bound-hit parameters or short contacts, preserves all three fractions, and contracts no monitored bond family beyond the allowed limit. I’m accepting that branch unchanged and moving to read-only replay plus exact-project exports.

One read-only diagnostic exposed a GSAS-II API quirk: calling its ID-indexing helper rewrote serialization metadata in `final.gpx` even though no scientific parameter changed. The accepted `polished.gpx` remains byte-for-byte intact. I’m restoring that exact file, removing the mutating helper from the export path, and will verify the project hash before and after the replay/export.

The exact-project replay succeeded on all 8,301 measured bins with no profile review flags. All exported CIFs pass the occupancy/contact screen and recover the expected compositions and symmetries. I’m writing the concise evidence/uncertainty report now, then I’ll restore and hash-check the untouched accepted project as the submitted `final.gpx`.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

- Ordered inverse-wolframite ZnWO₄: 83.11 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 12.05 wt%
